05:31.000 --> 05:36.000
gave us a little bit less than half of what we had actually planned.

05:36.000 --> 05:37.000
Uh-huh.

05:37.000 --> 05:45.000
That's really not as serious a short fall as it sounds just from the numbers because the

05:45.000 --> 05:48.000
readings were quite well distributed throughout the mission.

05:48.000 --> 05:49.000
Uh-huh.

05:49.000 --> 05:55.000
The general operation that we saw on the altimeter, uh-huh, the first several railroads were

05:55.000 --> 05:56.000
completely nominal.

05:56.000 --> 05:58.000
All of the elevation readings were valid.

05:58.000 --> 06:05.000
Then it began to fall off about 75 percent, 65 percent and down to about 60 percent except

06:05.000 --> 06:13.000
on the last data pass on Route 62 where it was only about, uh-huh, 10 percent effective.

06:13.000 --> 06:20.000
Generally what we seem to observe is that there would be one good shot and one bad shot

06:20.000 --> 06:25.000
and one good one bad and then maybe several good ones in a row.

06:25.000 --> 06:26.000
Uh-huh.

06:26.000 --> 06:32.000
We can talk about the reason for that but, uh-huh, that's more appropriately covered in these

06:32.000 --> 06:34.000
systems review tomorrow.

06:34.000 --> 06:35.000
Uh-huh.

06:35.000 --> 06:41.000
