06:41.000 --> 06:42.000
data points.

06:42.000 --> 06:48.200
So far as the, it's effect on the reduction of the photography is concerned that's absolutely

06:48.200 --> 06:49.200
inconsequential.

06:49.200 --> 06:56.000
So far as it's effect on the, uh, correlation between the tracking and gravity data and

06:56.000 --> 07:00.600
the, uh, profiles, it's a little bit, uh, more of concern.

07:00.600 --> 07:01.600
Uh-huh.

07:01.600 --> 07:04.700
They do have a little bit more smoothing to do between the data points that they got but

07:04.700 --> 07:09.800
essentially I don't think that it really hurts us so far as the scientific return, uh,

07:09.800 --> 07:11.800
from the mission is concerned.

07:11.800 --> 07:12.800
Uh-huh.

07:12.800 --> 07:19.800
I would like to say of just a word or so about the utilization of the photography, the,

07:19.800 --> 07:26.800
it has been proceeding much more slowly than I had anticipated in reducing the pictures

07:26.800 --> 07:28.800
from Apollo 15.

07:28.800 --> 07:35.300
However, the work which is being done by the ACIC and St. Louis is indicating that the

07:35.300 --> 07:41.660
photography, photographic reduction will provide, uh, positional coordinates of features

07:41.660 --> 07:48.940
on the lunar surface with the, uh, the accuracy of 10 to 12 meters in position and in elevation.

07:48.940 --> 07:53.740
And that seems to coincide very well with what we had predicted premission.

07:53.740 --> 07:59.220
So, uh, we are quite confident that, uh, that we're getting very good information from

07:59.220 --> 08:02.300
the pictures in that regard.

08:02.300 --> 08:09.980
The tracking data, uh, generally, uh, is consistent within an orbital pass but we do find

08:09.980 --> 08:17.100
uh, discrepancies of, uh, up to a kilometer between adjacent photographic passes so that

08:17.100 --> 08:23.900
the, uh, reduction of the photographs is giving us a much better tie between orbital, uh,

08:23.900 --> 08:28.300
passes than we get from the, from the tracking data, uh, itself.

08:29.300 --> 08:34.700
Consequently, we do expect eventually to come up with a, an internally consistent coordinate

08:34.700 --> 08:40.300
system reference system figure of the moon with an accuracy on the order of 12 to 15 meters.

08:40.300 --> 08:43.500
Uh, that's, uh, highly gratifying to me.

08:43.500 --> 08:50.980
Um, so far as the pan camera utilization is concerned, there have been some, uh, map

08:50.980 --> 08:58.140
compilations done, uh, the indicated precision of those is on the order of three meters,

08:58.140 --> 09:03.140
which again is about what we had expected, but that is a precision and not an accuracy

09:03.140 --> 09:07.860
number because, uh, the geometric problems with the pan camera photography.

09:07.860 --> 09:13.220
However, so far as the resolution of the pan camera is concerned, that has held up to just

09:13.220 --> 09:19.460
about what we had expected, uh, from one and a half to three meters at the, uh, sub

09:19.460 --> 09:24.620
vehicle point and decreasing off to about five to six meters at the limits of the, of the

09:24.620 --> 09:25.620
film.

09:25.620 --> 09:33.340
Uh, so, so far as, uh, our indications at the moment are that the results from 16 are quite

09:33.340 --> 09:34.340
satisfactory.

09:34.340 --> 09:40.220
We do have these problems that we have to resolve and the processing of the film.

09:40.220 --> 09:44.460
And, uh, once that is done, we will be able to say exactly what we did obtain.

09:44.460 --> 09:51.060
