16:57.120 --> 17:03.120
particularly on electric fields in the magneto tail.

17:03.120 --> 17:09.120
You know, on that, if they have those slides there, I'd like to briefly describe, uh,

17:09.120 --> 17:16.120
some of the results we got from Apollo 15 would hope to be similar for this one.

17:16.120 --> 17:28.120
Uh, our primary, uh, experiment was to examine particle shadow configurations in the magneto tail,

17:28.120 --> 17:36.120
uh, to be while we're passing through this region back here where we are in the Earth's magnetic field.

17:36.120 --> 17:47.120
And, uh, use that to examine the, uh, question of openness and access through the magneto tail to the magnetic field lines,

17:47.120 --> 17:53.120
and ultimately to the man Allen belts, where, uh,

17:53.120 --> 18:02.120
theory generally holds that, uh, the bound radiation must somehow come in and then be trapped and accelerated.

18:02.120 --> 18:10.120
The degree of connection back here with the interplanetary field has been in quite a bit of question,

18:10.120 --> 18:18.120
and briefly stated the Apollo 15 results indicate pretty clearly that at least most of the time,

18:18.120 --> 18:25.120
at the, uh, latitudes where we pass through the tail, of course we only have a couple of passes,

18:25.120 --> 18:34.120
that, uh, these, uh, field lines are, in fact, open out here, connect directly into the interplanetary field,

18:34.120 --> 18:41.120
and have direct access of solar cosmic ray particles.

18:41.120 --> 18:51.120
We have also observed, uh, the existence of a, uh, plasma sheet, which is known to form in closer to the Earth,

18:51.120 --> 18:56.120
to extend out to them in the distance.

18:56.120 --> 19:03.120
And, uh, at least a couple of occasions we have observed, uh, cross tail electric fields,

19:03.120 --> 19:11.120
which are important to theoretical models, uh, accounting for Aurora,

19:11.120 --> 19:17.120
and for solaration particles into the radiation belts.

19:17.120 --> 19:25.120
Uh, unexpected observation, which, uh, we found interesting was 30KV,

19:25.120 --> 19:33.120
or thereabouts protons in very large numbers, which we observed quite frequently back in the magnitude of tail,

19:33.120 --> 19:38.120
and also those are outside in magnitude of tail, in very similar fluxes.

19:38.120 --> 19:45.120
Our, uh, first feeling was that these must also be some, uh,

19:45.120 --> 19:50.120
component of the, uh, solar cosmic ray proton spectrum.

19:50.120 --> 19:59.120
Their, of course, very low energy would be stopped by even the thinnest piece of material,

19:59.120 --> 20:06.120
but the, uh, numbers of them, and the consistency of their flux density is making them,

20:06.120 --> 20:14.120
uh, very attractive as, probably being indicative, quite indicative of, uh, the source that they come from,

20:14.120 --> 20:16.120
and mechanism, bringing them.

20:16.120 --> 20:21.120
Uh, further examination, however, of the locations where we see them,

20:21.120 --> 20:26.120
and of the almost, uh, constant intensities that we see,

20:26.120 --> 20:32.120
uh, almost, for us, the conclusions that they must somehow,

20:32.120 --> 20:38.120
the protons from the, uh, outer van Allen belt region here,

20:38.120 --> 20:44.120
which are somehow, uh, coming loose from the Earth's field,

20:44.120 --> 20:50.120
and moving outward, and then getting on to the interplanetary fields,

20:50.120 --> 21:00.120
and then moving out to where we see them, and, uh, perhaps at any inverse process of the postulated, uh,

21:00.120 --> 21:08.120
process where we bring, uh, solar cosmic ray particles in from the sun to populate the van Allen belts initially.

21:08.120 --> 21:12.120
Uh, we're actually, uh, waiting, getting our data,

21:12.120 --> 21:19.120
and now our computer programs that, uh, berkeley and shape where we can examine this in detail,

21:19.120 --> 21:24.120
and if unfortunately we've been delayed in that.

21:24.120 --> 21:31.120
If I have the next slide, I'll show you one orbit of data from the telescopes,

21:31.120 --> 21:37.120
when these, uh, very, uh, steady fluxes of protons were present,

21:37.120 --> 21:44.120
and, uh, during this period, and actually for a period of, uh, a couple of orbits,

21:44.120 --> 21:48.120
earlier, these fluxes were almost constant.

21:48.120 --> 21:51.120
There is some, uh, changing here.

21:51.120 --> 21:57.120
There, there, there is a very, very slight shadowing of protons, not very much at all.

21:57.120 --> 22:03.120
This is most in electron phenomena, but then these particles essentially turn off,

22:03.120 --> 22:10.120
as if somebody closed a valve, and this is characteristic of the way they behave.

22:10.120 --> 22:17.120
When they appear, they're suddenly there, and when they disappear, they're gone just as fast.

22:17.120 --> 22:28.120
And, uh, we're very hopeful that we'll be able to make some sense out of what turns these things on and off,

22:28.120 --> 22:34.120
and determine what, where they're coming from, and what the mechanism is,

22:34.120 --> 22:38.120
and presumably now in the magnetosphere.

