Last subject, we'll be the transponder.

41:29.120 --> 41:38.120
Oh, this is the gravity experiment.

41:38.120 --> 41:43.120
And we monitor the gravity by just monitoring the

41:43.120 --> 41:47.120
velocity of the spacecraft, or the lab,

41:47.120 --> 41:50.120
or the sub-satellite.

41:50.120 --> 41:53.120
Of course, on this particular mission, we lost our

41:53.120 --> 41:56.120
limb data on impact because it started crumbling.

41:56.120 --> 41:58.120
And so that data was lost.

41:58.120 --> 42:03.120
We do have the CSM in the low altitude orbit,

42:03.120 --> 42:06.120
which is very interesting data.

42:27.120 --> 42:30.120
Here is the orbital track.

42:30.120 --> 42:35.120
And hopefully, I can get this aligned.

42:35.120 --> 42:37.120
Not like this.

42:37.120 --> 42:42.120
This profile just below it is the gravity anomaly

42:42.120 --> 42:44.120
that was detected.

42:44.120 --> 42:49.120
This line right here represents zero gravity center.

42:49.120 --> 42:50.120
I just got to get equilibrium.

42:50.120 --> 42:52.120
And anything below it, of course,

42:52.120 --> 42:56.120
would be negative gravity deviations.

42:56.120 --> 42:58.120
Here we are at Tolomeus,

42:58.120 --> 43:02.120
going essentially over the center with the track.

43:02.120 --> 43:04.120
And we can see the large negative anomaly.

43:04.120 --> 43:08.120
Again, almost 100 milligales.

43:08.120 --> 43:11.120
Here we see some highland material,

43:11.120 --> 43:13.120
where we have a positive.

43:13.120 --> 43:16.120
Then we drop that into a relative low

43:16.120 --> 43:18.120
in between these two old craters,

43:18.120 --> 43:20.120
the park is now the technius.

43:20.120 --> 43:22.120
And then a high in here.

43:22.120 --> 43:26.120
At this point, this is the day cart,

43:26.120 --> 43:29.120
the landing site, and we're actually at about a 50 milligale

43:29.120 --> 43:31.120
negative anomaly in that region.

43:31.120 --> 43:34.120
There's some nice correlations here with the

43:34.120 --> 43:36.120
laser altimetry measurements.

43:36.120 --> 43:39.120
I'll show them in the next profile.

43:39.120 --> 43:40.120
But this is continuous.

43:40.120 --> 43:42.120
I've just taken one small section here.

43:42.120 --> 43:45.120
We have this thing from limb to limb.

43:45.120 --> 43:50.120
So about 110 longitude plus to 110 minus.

43:50.120 --> 43:56.120
And essentially, three revs and revs three through rev.

43:56.120 --> 44:00.120
11, eight revs.

44:00.120 --> 44:03.120
Eight revs of data that are pretty good.

44:03.120 --> 44:06.120
And then they started their station keeping

44:06.120 --> 44:09.120
in a data kind of garbage stuff a lot.

44:09.120 --> 44:11.120
All of the new things going on.

44:11.120 --> 44:19.120
I don't even plan out one kind of interesting thing here.

44:19.120 --> 44:22.120
Notice that Tylenase here has an anomaly much lower

44:22.120 --> 44:23.120
than Nubium.

44:23.120 --> 44:25.120
I'll note Nubium, which is this region right in here,

44:25.120 --> 44:27.120
this marinubium right in here.

44:27.120 --> 44:31.120
It's still at about minus 50 milligales.

44:41.120 --> 44:45.120
Here's an altimeter profile.

44:45.120 --> 44:50.120
And going right to the Nubium Tylenase region again,

44:50.120 --> 44:54.120
we see that Nubium elevation here is some,

44:54.120 --> 44:57.120
by the way, the scale here is two kilometers

44:57.120 --> 44:59.120
per heavy line.

44:59.120 --> 45:01.120
So we've dropped down here almost two kilometers

45:01.120 --> 45:04.120
from the floor of Tylenase to the floor of Nubium.

45:04.120 --> 45:09.120
Yet the gravity anomaly is just the opposite

45:09.120 --> 45:12.120
that Tylenase is some 50 milligales lower

45:12.120 --> 45:15.120
than the Nubium region.

45:15.120 --> 45:17.120
And another interesting point is,

45:17.120 --> 45:20.120
Fred on these ACIC maps, Nubium is showing

45:20.120 --> 45:24.120
a kilometer higher than Tylenase.

45:24.120 --> 45:27.120
And here we are, two kilometers lower.

45:27.120 --> 45:29.120
So we're talking about three kilometers of scrapancy

45:29.120 --> 45:31.120
in just that little area right there.

45:31.120 --> 45:33.120
So I think some of these guys were talking about the right thing

45:33.120 --> 45:36.120
when they said we really didn't know the altitudes.

45:36.120 --> 45:40.120
I think that shows up quite dramatically

45:40.120 --> 45:45.120
when you look at the low sun angle view

45:45.120 --> 45:48.120
as you approach the terminator, the things that were in Nubium

45:48.120 --> 45:53.120
didn't show up until significantly after we anticipated.

45:53.120 --> 45:55.120
We missed our times on those things, a great deal.

45:55.120 --> 45:58.120
And I could only be called by having a discrepancy

45:58.120 --> 45:59.120
in our relative heights.

45:59.120 --> 46:02.120
Yeah, this is not just, this is red 28.

46:02.120 --> 46:06.120
We've looked at, there are five real good tracking,

46:06.120 --> 46:08.120
laser tracking passes over this region.

46:08.120 --> 46:10.120
And we've looked at three of them.

46:10.120 --> 46:13.120
And all three show the same consistency of the drop there.

46:13.120 --> 46:16.120
Other interesting thing here, Prokalarm,

46:16.120 --> 46:18.120
Tranquilatatus, Fricundatatus,

46:18.120 --> 46:20.120
are all seem to be about on the same level.

46:20.120 --> 46:23.120
Smithy I, again, being below about four and a half

46:23.120 --> 46:26.120
kilometers, that was the same thing on the Apollo 15

46:26.120 --> 46:28.120
when we passed over it.

46:28.120 --> 46:30.120
It was some four and a half kilometers below,

46:30.120 --> 46:32.120
as mean radius here.

46:32.120 --> 46:35.120
And we're still referencing this to a 1738,

46:35.120 --> 46:40.120
well, 0.1 radius off the CG.

46:40.120 --> 46:44.120
If you take this stuff and start fitting it

46:44.120 --> 46:49.120
to an optical center with a CG offset

46:49.120 --> 46:53.120
that you do indeed get the two kilometers shift again

46:53.120 --> 46:57.120
of the CG being closer to the Earth by some two kilometers.

46:57.120 --> 47:01.120
Let's say that Tranquilatatus is about two and a half kilometers

47:01.120 --> 47:03.120
lower than the car.

47:03.120 --> 47:04.120
That's right.

47:04.120 --> 47:06.120
Yeah, here's the landing site right in here.

47:06.120 --> 47:09.120
And this is where we had a negative gravity anomaly in here.

47:09.120 --> 47:11.120
Well, kind of according to this name,

47:11.120 --> 47:15.120
this situation, because it looks like a topographic load.

47:27.120 --> 47:30.120
This is the backside.

47:30.120 --> 47:33.120
We had some, these are the limbs where the data was missing.

47:33.120 --> 47:36.120
These are taken from those data taken from that thrift printout

47:36.120 --> 47:37.120
that we get in real time.

47:37.120 --> 47:39.120
And there's gaps in the data.

47:39.120 --> 47:44.120
These will be filled in once they get the station tapes delivered.

47:44.120 --> 47:47.120
Or we can see the crater hurt sprung here very evidently

47:47.120 --> 47:49.120
with the central peak or something in it.

47:49.120 --> 47:55.120
You don't have any data in that 120 degree region.

47:55.120 --> 47:58.120
Not now, which we should have next week or so.

47:58.120 --> 47:59.120
I'm going to get the tapes here.

48:03.120 --> 48:07.120
One of the things that, well, on Apollo 15,

48:07.120 --> 48:10.120
there is a definite, well, centered about 180.

48:10.120 --> 48:14.120
There was a definite big trough in here.

48:14.120 --> 48:16.120
We went down about some four and a half kilometers.

48:16.120 --> 48:18.120
It was very jagged.

48:18.120 --> 48:20.120
But it was centered just about here.

48:20.120 --> 48:23.120
On Apollo 16, now we do not really see that.

48:23.120 --> 48:27.120
We see this highland material here, which we had on 15.

48:27.120 --> 48:34.120
That started about in here and showed a marked high region in there.

48:34.120 --> 48:37.120
And this is holding on 16 also.

48:37.120 --> 48:44.120
So this is kind of consistent with 15.

48:44.120 --> 48:48.120
Here's another, this is orbit 38 on the backside.

48:48.120 --> 48:52.120
I'm just kind of lay it there to show you some of the consistency.

48:52.120 --> 48:56.120
This is 10 orbits later.

48:56.120 --> 48:58.120
The profile is almost there.

48:58.120 --> 49:00.120
Smith, the eye.

49:00.120 --> 49:02.120
Oh, here we have some data in that.

49:02.120 --> 49:04.120
So there is some there is your data.

49:04.120 --> 49:06.120
10.

49:06.120 --> 49:09.120
Well, you're looking for some particular feature right there.

49:09.120 --> 49:11.120
You're looking for something around 105.

49:11.120 --> 49:13.120
105.

49:13.120 --> 49:29.120
I do have some data on the sub satellite from Apollo 15.

49:29.120 --> 49:31.120
But I don't know if that's the time to really show you.

49:31.120 --> 49:33.120
I think I've shown enough stuff on 16.

49:33.120 --> 49:34.120
Yeah.

49:34.120 --> 49:36.120
Well, I like to stick to 16.

49:36.120 --> 49:40.120
Any of the problems we have or any of the data that we got for the group.

49:40.120 --> 49:44.120
Well, I'm very happy with all the data we've gotten on 16.

49:44.120 --> 49:50.120
And sub satellite on 16, like Larry mentioned just before me.

49:50.120 --> 49:52.120
It's pretty grim.

49:52.120 --> 49:56.120
Although there wasn't no D solution just run out about an hour ago,

49:56.120 --> 50:07.120
where the last state vector was used as the position for running out the lifetime program.

50:07.120 --> 50:12.120
If we believe that 15-8 model that we had used initially back there when we

50:12.120 --> 50:16.120
objected that thing, says if this thing should come back up.

50:16.120 --> 50:18.120
Still says it's not going to crash.

50:18.120 --> 50:22.120
But this would be a very interesting thing to watch the next couple days.

50:22.120 --> 50:23.120
Right.

50:23.120 --> 50:26.120
That's the right thing to hold this as long as I'm upgraded.

50:26.120 --> 50:28.120
Well, it's just...

50:28.120 --> 50:32.120
I had to talk about that.

50:32.120 --> 50:36.120
And I don't know where he can speak for later or not.

50:36.120 --> 50:41.120
But I told him it's 50 pounds and where they can actually see it in fact of 50 pounds.

50:41.120 --> 50:45.120
I think that's all the way to the central station.

50:45.120 --> 50:53.120
Oh, that should hit about maybe some 20 or 30 degrees,

50:53.120 --> 50:56.120
west line to two, then five or six degrees north.

50:56.120 --> 50:59.120
That right thing, something like that.

50:59.120 --> 51:03.120
Five or six, no, that's latitude, and about 30 west.

51:03.120 --> 51:04.120
That's what it looks like in a...

51:04.120 --> 51:06.120
That linear extrapolation.

51:06.120 --> 51:07.120
Okay.

51:07.120 --> 51:10.120
That's reasonable.

51:10.120 --> 51:12.120
Well, thank you, Bill.

51:12.120 --> 51:14.120
Thank you, gentlemen, for attending.

51:14.120 --> 51:17.120
This concludes our scientific debrief.

51:17.120 --> 51:21.120
And I'd like to thank the crew for giving their time up to come on over to.

51:21.120 --> 51:24.120
Give us some information and also learn some things that will happen.

51:24.120 --> 51:25.120
Okay.

51:25.120 --> 51:27.120
Let me say something for the crew.

51:27.120 --> 51:31.120
I want to commend Ken for the way that he operated the Sim Bay.

51:31.120 --> 51:41.120
I don't think you'll ever find a guy who's either more interested in it or more aware of what the operational problems were and who could do a better job.

51:41.120 --> 51:44.120
He really did an outstanding job and I think he's well done.

51:44.120 --> 51:48.120
The other thing I'd like to say is, since I've been on this program,

51:48.120 --> 51:56.120
I've been continually impressed with the importance of what we're doing in our support of you guys.

51:56.120 --> 52:04.120
These experiments, they look way out right now and they're difficult for the man in the street to relate to.

52:04.120 --> 52:15.120
But my feeling is from some of the things I've seen here and from the thrust of science and technology in this country that in our lifetime,

52:15.120 --> 52:22.120
and I want to say when, but I imagine it's going to be a damn site sooner than anybody even here in this room can imagine,

52:22.120 --> 52:28.120
they're going to be practical applications of some of these discoveries that have been made that will affect everyone of us.

52:28.120 --> 52:30.120
And I think you're to be congratulated.

52:30.120 --> 52:35.120
The other thing is that I get out of all this, that I think the United States ought to be spending two to three times.

52:35.120 --> 52:40.120
The amount of money that we are spending on basic research and development and applied research and development.

52:40.120 --> 52:47.120
And I think if you really look at the big picture of our energy requirements for clean energy in the future,

52:47.120 --> 52:52.120
in order to improve the life of human beings on this planet, we ought to do that and get on with it.

52:52.120 --> 52:53.120
Thank you.

52:58.120 --> 53:02.120
During the next couple of months,

53:02.120 --> 53:14.120
we'll probably have an opportunity to talk to a lot of people that help to have some influence or shape the pattern of our program and related programs.

53:14.120 --> 53:20.120
And I would appreciate it very much and through we all could use the data.

53:20.120 --> 53:29.120
As you find out things, we realize that you have preliminary data today and a lot of the things that are only hints and suggestions of things to come.

53:30.120 --> 53:36.120
I would personally appreciate it very much if you give us a call periodically and tell us what you found.

53:36.120 --> 53:45.120
It's too easy for us to go through here at the end of a week or so, walk off and never really know what happened and never know what kind of data came out.

53:45.120 --> 53:50.120
And I think that makes us very poor salesman.

53:50.120 --> 53:56.120
I think you could help us do the job to kind of feedback the quality of the data that you've been getting.

53:56.120 --> 54:04.120
If you could keep us informed, please don't ever feel like you're interfering with our operation or don't be inhibited to call us.

54:04.120 --> 54:08.120
If we can't come to the phone and take the data or talk to you about it,

54:08.120 --> 54:12.120
the Secretary will take the number we'll be glad to call you back.

54:12.120 --> 54:18.120
And if there's any time when you have some question about the data, it doesn't matter how trivial it may seem to you.

54:18.120 --> 54:25.120
If you want to look at some data and you're curious to know whether we've ever seen a particular observation or whether the spacecraft was moving at the time,

54:25.120 --> 54:31.120
your data was taken or I don't care how far out it might seem to you.

54:31.120 --> 54:35.120
Questions are pretty cheap and not asking questions can be very expensive.

54:35.120 --> 54:41.120
And so please feel like our job isn't finished until you guys have all information you can use.

54:41.120 --> 54:44.120
And I would like for you to feel like you can call anytime.

54:44.120 --> 54:45.120
Thank you very much.

54:45.120 --> 54:49.120
I'd like to say, give you my thanks also.

54:49.120 --> 54:58.120
I was very pleased with the way all the gear worked, especially my part on the lunar surface and part I saw of Ken and orbit.

54:58.120 --> 55:06.120
It was just a real pleasure to have him placed all this stuff for all you people and glad it's working so well.

55:06.120 --> 55:10.120
And thanks for the opportunity of going if you can gin up another one.

55:10.120 --> 55:13.120
I'll be glad to go along again. Thank you.

55:19.120 --> 55:22.120
Thank you.

